WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.240
Does a signal from the axon tip really keep the cell body alive?

2
00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:09.500
And what actually makes the trip?

3
00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:16.030
First, radioactive NGF placed in a target built up in the matching ganglion on the

4
00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:20.530
same side. In the chick, the motor neurons beside it stayed unlabelled.

5
00:00:21.333 --> 00:00:26.133
But label shows only where radioactivity went, not whether it mattered for survival.

6
00:00:27.217 --> 00:00:29.537
Robert Campenot's dish answered that.

7
00:00:30.297 --> 00:00:33.517
Sealed dividers split it into chambers whose fluids do not mix.

8
00:00:34.517 --> 00:00:36.237
Cell bodies sit in the centre.

9
00:00:37.177 --> 00:00:39.817
Their axons grow under the seal into side chambers.

10
00:00:40.867 --> 00:00:42.867
Give the cell bodies no NGF.

11
00:00:43.827 --> 00:00:46.067
Let their axons reach a chamber that has it.

12
00:00:46.807 --> 00:00:47.907
Do the cell bodies live?

13
00:00:54.317 --> 00:01:00.357
Many survive. A signal from the distal axons, the far ends near the terminals, can

14
00:01:00.357 --> 00:01:01.697
keep the cell body alive.

15
00:01:05.167 --> 00:01:10.687
With NGF only on distal axons, CREB became phosphorylated in the nuclei.

16
00:01:11.547 --> 00:01:14.527
CREB is a transcription factor that switches genes on.

17
00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:18.450
That needed TrkA kinase activity in the cell body.

18
00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:21.670
But was that the same receptor, carried from the tip?

19
00:01:22.683 --> 00:01:23.943
So how did it travel?

20
00:01:26.183 --> 00:01:29.783
The NGF and TrkA complex itself is carried the whole way.

21
00:01:30.767 --> 00:01:36.327
Relay. Each stretch of axon switches on the next, like a row of dominoes.

22
00:01:36.967 --> 00:01:38.807
No one molecule makes the whole trip.

23
00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:41.570
Blocking dynein stops survival.

24
00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:43.510
Does that prove a courier?

25
00:01:49.933 --> 00:01:54.673
No. If each relay step needed dynein, the relay would stop too.

26
00:01:55.553 --> 00:01:57.093
This block cannot tell them apart.

27
00:02:00.483 --> 00:02:05.343
So block TrkA's kinase only in the middle stretch, with an inhibitor applied only

28
00:02:05.343 --> 00:02:07.723
there. What does each model predict?

29
00:02:14.183 --> 00:02:17.763
A relay that needs TrkA kinase in each stretch would break at the gap.

30
00:02:18.543 --> 00:02:21.203
A carried complex could pass through and signal again.

31
00:02:22.183 --> 00:02:25.723
The signal still reached the cell bodies, and the neurons survived.

32
00:02:26.703 --> 00:02:28.903
NGF also had to be taken into the cell.

33
00:02:32.767 --> 00:02:34.707
Two results keep the question open.

34
00:02:35.767 --> 00:02:40.067
TrkA in the cell bodies was phosphorylated within a minute, far faster than

35
00:02:40.067 --> 00:02:41.067
endosomes move.

36
00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:47.690
And NGF fixed to beads supported survival with little or no NGF transport.

37
00:02:48.733 --> 00:02:51.833
Takeaway: for survival, the evidence favours a courier.

38
00:02:52.793 --> 00:02:55.313
Whether a faster signal also helps is still open.
